## His life

The myths of his life, from his birth to his apotheosis, with where each
is told. He also takes part in two myths of the gods: the war of the
Giants, which the gods can win only with a mortal's help, and the freeing
of Prometheus, on the way to the Hesperides.

```hql id=life hidden
import myth.kb
import myth.stories

kb.myths() | filter(m => m.features("heracles"))
```

```hql#eval
import myth.kb
import myth.stories

works = kb.works()

born = life | find(m => m.id == "heracles-birth") | map(m => m.seq) | unwrap_or(0)

life
| filter(m => m.seq >= born and not m.id.starts_with("labour-"))
| map(m => {myth: m.title, story: m.summary, told: m.sources(works) | join("; ")})
| Table(columns = ["myth", "story", "told"])
```
<!-- hql:output hash=cf4875726779 status=ok -->
| myth                                       | story                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    | told                                                                                   |
| ------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| The birth of Heracles                      | Zeus swears that the descendant of his born that day will rule; Hera delays Alcmene's labour so that Eurystheus is born first.                                                                                                           | Homer, Iliad 19.95–133; Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 2.4.8; Hesiod, Theogony 943–944    |
| The infant Heracles strangles the serpents | Hera sends two serpents to the cradle; the infant Heracles strangles them.                                                                                                                                                               | Pindar, Nemean Odes 1.33–72; Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 2.4.8                         |
| The madness of Heracles                    | Driven mad by Hera, Heracles kills his children by Megara. In Apollodorus the Pythia then sends him to serve Eurystheus for twelve years and perform ten labours; in Euripides the madness comes after the labours, and Megara dies too. | Euripides, Heracles 822–1015; Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 2.4.12                       |
| The death of Heracles                      | Deianira sends a robe smeared with the centaur Nessus's poisoned blood, thinking it a love charm; in agony Heracles has himself burned on a pyre on Oeta.                                                                                | Sophocles, Women of Trachis 531–1278; Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 2.7.7                |
| Heracles among the gods                    | Made immortal, Heracles is reconciled with Hera and marries Hebe; Odysseus sees only his image among the dead.                                                                                                                           | Hesiod, Theogony 950–955; Homer, Odyssey 11.601–604; Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 2.7.7 |
<!-- /hql:output -->

## The twelve labours

In Pseudo-Apollodorus's order, numbered as a Greek would number them, in
alphabetic numerals: `12.numeral()` is `ιβʹ`, by the `greek` package.
Apollodorus says Eurystheus first set ten, and refused to count the hydra,
because Iolaus helped, and the stables, because Augeas promised payment.
The metopes of the Temple of Zeus at Olympia, about 460 BCE, are the
earliest known set of twelve ([[evidence]]).

```hql#eval
import greek
import myth.kb
import myth.stories

life
| filter(m => m.id.starts_with("labour-"))
| sort(by = m => m.seq)
| enumerate
| map(p => {number: (p.first + 1).numeral(), labour: p.second.title, place: p.second.place.unwrap_or(""), story: p.second.summary})
| Table(columns = ["number", "labour", "place", "story"])
```
<!-- hql:output hash=a1a121f7a3ee status=ok -->
| number | labour                       | place          | story                                                                                                                                                    |
| ------ | ---------------------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| αʹ     | The Nemean lion              | Nemea          | The lion, born of Typhon, cannot be wounded, so Heracles chokes it and carries it to Mycenae.                                                            |
| βʹ     | The Lernaean hydra           | Lerna          | Iolaus sears each neck as Heracles cuts off a head; Eurystheus does not count the labour because Heracles had help.                                      |
| γʹ     | The Ceryneian hind           | Ceryneia       | Heracles hunts the golden-horned hind, sacred to Artemis, for a whole year.                                                                              |
| δʹ     | The Erymanthian boar         | Erymanthus     | Heracles drives the boar into deep snow and brings it back alive.                                                                                        |
| εʹ     | The stables of Augeas        | Elis           | Heracles cleans the stables in one day by turning the Alpheus and the Peneus through them; Eurystheus does not count it because Augeas promised payment. |
| ϛʹ     | The Stymphalian birds        | Stymphalus     | Heracles scares the birds from their marsh with bronze clappers made by Hephaestus and given by Athena, then shoots them.                                |
| ζʹ     | The Cretan bull              | Crete          | Heracles brings the bull of Minos to Eurystheus, who lets it go; it roams to Marathon.                                                                   |
| ηʹ     | The mares of Diomedes        | Thrace         | Heracles takes the man-eating mares of the Thracian king Diomedes.                                                                                       |
| θʹ     | The belt of Hippolyte        | Themiscyra     | Heracles fetches the belt of the Amazon queen for Eurystheus's daughter Admete.                                                                          |
| ιʹ     | The cattle of Geryon         | Erytheia       | Heracles crosses the Ocean in the cup of Helios, sets up the Pillars at the strait, kills Orthus and Geryon and drives the cattle to Greece.             |
| ιαʹ    | The apples of the Hesperides | the Hesperides | On the way Heracles frees Prometheus; in one account he takes the sky from Atlas while Atlas fetches the apples.                                         |
| ιβʹ    | Cerberus                     | the underworld | With Hades's leave and without weapons, Heracles brings Cerberus up from the underworld and shows him to Eurystheus.                                     |
